          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The original motivation behind condensed infinity-groupoids is to create well-behaved categories of mathematical structures such as condensed
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/HZ"} HZ
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/module+spectra"} module spectra
            in which one could do derived analytic geometry using category-theoretic methods without resorting to not-so-well behaved categories of topological spaces.
          %p
            However,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Corfield"} David Corfield
            offers another motivation for condensed infinity-groupoids in the blog post
            %a{:href => "https://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2020/03/pyknoticity_versus_cohesivenes.html#c057721"} Pyknoticity vs Cohesiveness
            on the nCafé, as a way to extend
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesion"} cohesion
            to the
            = succeed "-adic" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            = succeed "):" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/condensed+cohesion"} condensed cohesion
          %blockquote
            %p
              Mathematics rather than physics is the subject of chapter 5 of my book, where I’m presenting
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+homotopy+type+theory"} cohesive HoTT
              as a means to gain some kind of conceptual traction over the vast terrain that is modern geometry. However I’m aware that there are some apparent limitations, problems with ‘
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              \-adic’ forms of cohesion, cohesion in algebraic geometry, and so on. In the briefest note (p. 158) I mention the closely related pyknotic and condensed approaches of, respectively, (Barwick and Haine) and (Clausen and Scholze).
          %h2#definition  Definition
          %p
            A
            %strong condensed infinity-groupoid
            or
            %strong condensed anima
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hypercomplete+%28infinity%2C1%29-topos"} hypercomplete
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-sheaf"} (infinity,1)-sheaf
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-groupoids"} infinity-groupoids
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pro-%C3%A9tale+%28infinity%2C1%29-site"} pro-étale (infinity,1)-site
            of the point, small relative to a universe
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒰
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}
          %p
            Equivalently, it is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-functor"} (infinity,1)-functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi ProfiniteSpaces
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} op
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mn ∞
                  %msub
                    %mi Grpd
                    %mi 𝒰
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T:ProfiniteSpaces^\op \longrightarrow \infty Grpd_\mathcal{U}
          %p
            from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} opposite
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} (infinity,1)-category
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/profinite+spaces"} profinite spaces
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} (infinity,1)-category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Infinity-Grpd"} Infinity-Grpd
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-groupoids"} infinity-groupoids
            which are small relative to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universe"} universe
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒰
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝟘
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝟙
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(\mathbb{0}) \to \mathbb{1}
          %p and
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo +
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(S + S^{'}) \to T(S) \times T(S^{'})
          %p
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+equivalences"} homotopy equivalences
            for any profinite space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^{'}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} lim
                    %mi Δ
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(S) \to  \operatorname{lim}_{\Delta} T(S_\bullet)
          %p
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+equivalence"} homotopy equivalence
            for any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hypercover"} hypercover
            of profinite spaces
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mo •
                    %mo →
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_\bullet \to S
          %p
            When restricted to the site of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extremally+disconnected+topological+space"} extremally disconnected
            compact Hausdorff spaces, hyperdescent is automatic, so the simplest equivalent definition of a condensed infinity-groupoid is simply a finite-product-preserving
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-functor"} (infinity,1)-functor
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi ExtrDisc
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} op
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mn ∞
                    %msub
                      %mi Grpd
                      %mi 𝒰
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T:ExtrDisc^\op \longrightarrow \infty Grpd_\mathcal{U}
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#BarwickHaine19"} Barwick-Haine, corollary 2.4.4
          %h2#properties Properties
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} (infinity,1)-category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
